return json_decode( <<<'SANDWICHJSON'
{
 "pages": [
  {
   "slug": "thai-massage",
   "label": "Thai Massage",
   "old_research": "<!-- wp:uagb/advanced-heading {\"block_id\":\"7e4a91c2\",\"classMigrate\":true,\"headingDescToggle\":false,\"headingDescPosition\":\"above-heading\",\"headingAlign\":\"center\",\"separatorColor\":\"var(--ast-global-color-0)\",\"seperatorStyle\":\"solid\",\"seperatorPosition\":\"above-heading\",\"separatorWidth\":64,\"separatorWidthType\":\"px\",\"headSpace\":0,\"separatorSpace\":14,\"blockTopPadding\":100,\"blockRightPadding\":220,\"blockLeftPadding\":220,\"blockBottomPadding\":28,\"blockTopPaddingTablet\":80,\"blockRightPaddingTablet\":40,\"blockLeftPaddingTablet\":40,\"blockBottomPaddingTablet\":24,\"blockTopPaddingMobile\":60,\"blockRightPaddingMobile\":0,\"blockLeftPaddingMobile\":0,\"blockBottomPaddingMobile\":20} -->\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-advanced-heading uagb-block-7e4a91c2\"><div class=\"uagb-separator\"></div><h2 class=\"uagb-heading-text\">What the Research Says About Thai Massage</h2></div>\n<!-- /wp:uagb/advanced-heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Research on traditional Thai massage is still limited. Most studies are small, many compare Thai massage with another approach rather than with no massage, and most follow people for only a few weeks. The summaries below describe what the studies reported. They are general information, not medical advice or a promise of any result, and people respond differently.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:details {\"showContent\":true,\"style\":{\"border\":{\"width\":\"1px\",\"style\":\"solid\",\"color\":\"var(\\u002d\\u002dts-rule)\",\"radius\":\"4px\"},\"color\":{\"background\":\"var(\\u002d\\u002dts-panel)\"},\"spacing\":{\"padding\":{\"top\":\"0.85rem\",\"right\":\"1.1rem\",\"bottom\":\"0.85rem\",\"left\":\"1.1rem\"},\"margin\":{\"bottom\":\"0.7rem\"}},\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"600\"}}} -->\n<details class=\"wp-block-details has-border-color has-background\" style=\"border-color:var(--ts-rule);border-width:1px;border-style:solid;border-radius:4px;background-color:var(--ts-panel);padding-top:0.85rem;padding-right:1.1rem;padding-bottom:0.85rem;padding-left:1.1rem;margin-bottom:0.7rem;font-weight:600\" open><summary>Reviews, and What They Say About Limits</summary>\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"style\":{\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"400\"}}} -->\n<p style=\"font-weight:400\">A 2015 systematic review of six studies of traditional Thai massage in people with long-lasting pain found that every study reported less pain after a course of sessions. The review noted that the reduction appeared to last for up to 15 weeks, and its authors said more research is needed to learn how Thai massage compares with control conditions over the longer term.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25682523/\">[2]</a></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"style\":{\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"400\"}}} -->\n<p style=\"font-weight:400\">A 2012 review of six studies of Swedish and Thai massage for low-back pain reported that the two appeared to be about equally helpful, and noted that weaknesses in the studies mean more direct comparisons are needed.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23059437/\">[3]</a></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"style\":{\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"400\"}}} -->\n<p style=\"font-weight:400\">Reviews of massage in general are cautious, too. A 2015 Cochrane review of 25 trials with 3,096 people with low-back pain found some short-term benefits, but the studies were small and of low quality, and the review’s authors said they had “very little confidence” in the results.<a href=\"https://www.cochrane.org/evidence/CD001929_massage-low-back-pain\">[4]</a> A 2024 Cochrane review of 33 studies with 1,994 people with neck pain found that massage probably made little or no difference compared with a placebo massage after about 12 weeks (low-certainty evidence), although a subgroup analysis favored higher doses of eight or more sessions of at least 30 minutes over four weeks.<a href=\"https://www.cochrane.org/evidence/CD004871_massage-neck-pain\">[5]</a></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"style\":{\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"400\"}}} -->\n<p style=\"font-weight:400\">The U.S. National Center for Complementary and Integrative Health notes that the risk of harm from massage appears to be low, although rare serious side effects such as a blood clot, nerve injury or bone fracture have been reported, and it advises against using massage to put off seeing a health care provider about a medical problem.<a href=\"https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/massage-therapy-what-you-need-to-know\">[6]</a></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n</details>\n<!-- /wp:details -->\n\n<!-- wp:details {\"style\":{\"border\":{\"width\":\"1px\",\"style\":\"solid\",\"color\":\"var(\\u002d\\u002dts-rule)\",\"radius\":\"4px\"},\"color\":{\"background\":\"var(\\u002d\\u002dts-panel)\"},\"spacing\":{\"padding\":{\"top\":\"0.85rem\",\"right\":\"1.1rem\",\"bottom\":\"0.85rem\",\"left\":\"1.1rem\"},\"margin\":{\"bottom\":\"0.7rem\"}},\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"600\"}}} -->\n<details class=\"wp-block-details has-border-color has-background\" style=\"border-color:var(--ts-rule);border-width:1px;border-style:solid;border-radius:4px;background-color:var(--ts-panel);padding-top:0.85rem;padding-right:1.1rem;padding-bottom:0.85rem;padding-left:1.1rem;margin-bottom:0.7rem;font-weight:600\"><summary>Back Pain</summary>\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"style\":{\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"400\"}}} -->\n<p style=\"font-weight:400\">Several small trials have compared Thai massage with another approach for low-back pain. In a hospital trial in Bangkok, 120 adults had Thai massage or joint mobilization twice a week for four weeks, and both groups improved by similar amounts on pain and everyday-activity scores. The trial had no comparison group that received neither, so it cannot show how much of the change was due to the sessions themselves.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28690703/\">[7]</a> In 140 older adults, Thai massage alone and Thai massage with an herbal compress led to similar improvements over 15 weeks, so the compress added no clear benefit.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31987237/\">[8]</a> In another trial of 140 adults, adding acupuncture to court-type Thai massage made no clear difference after one month, and both groups reported less pain and more movement.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42142427/\">[9]</a></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"style\":{\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"400\"}}} -->\n<p style=\"font-weight:400\">An earlier trial that compared Thai and Swedish massage for back pain with tender spots in the muscles found that pain fell by more than half in both groups after three weeks and stayed lower for up to a month, with no significant difference between them.<a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jbmt.2005.02.001\">[10]</a> A preliminary trial of 18 military personnel with back pain found less pain after both Thai massage and acupuncture, with one pain-questionnaire score lower after acupuncture.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21299184/\">[11]</a></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"style\":{\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"400\"}}} -->\n<p style=\"font-weight:400\">Trials that used a sham (inactive) device or rest as the comparison also reported differences. In 50 adults with upper-back pain, one 30-minute session of Thai massage was followed by larger reductions in pain, muscle tension and muscle activity than a sham device.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27670864/\">[12]</a> In 36 adults with back pain, one 30-minute session was followed by lower self-reported pain, anxiety and muscle tension and better flexibility than resting on a bed.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21147414/\">[13]</a></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n</details>\n<!-- /wp:details -->\n\n<!-- wp:details {\"style\":{\"border\":{\"width\":\"1px\",\"style\":\"solid\",\"color\":\"var(\\u002d\\u002dts-rule)\",\"radius\":\"4px\"},\"color\":{\"background\":\"var(\\u002d\\u002dts-panel)\"},\"spacing\":{\"padding\":{\"top\":\"0.85rem\",\"right\":\"1.1rem\",\"bottom\":\"0.85rem\",\"left\":\"1.1rem\"},\"margin\":{\"bottom\":\"0.7rem\"}},\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"600\"}}} -->\n<details class=\"wp-block-details has-border-color has-background\" style=\"border-color:var(--ts-rule);border-width:1px;border-style:solid;border-radius:4px;background-color:var(--ts-panel);padding-top:0.85rem;padding-right:1.1rem;padding-bottom:0.85rem;padding-left:1.1rem;margin-bottom:0.7rem;font-weight:600\"><summary>Neck, Shoulders, Arms and Feet</summary>\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"style\":{\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"400\"}}} -->\n<p style=\"font-weight:400\">In 45 adults with neck pain, eight sessions of Thai massage or of a muscle energy technique over two weeks were each followed by more improvement in pain and neck movement than in a control group, with no clear difference between the two techniques.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34391301/\">[14]</a></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"style\":{\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"400\"}}} -->\n<p style=\"font-weight:400\">Two trials compared Thai massage with exercise for shoulder pain. In 78 adults with shoulder-blade pain, four weeks of Thai massage or Thai boxing exercise both reduced pain, and Thai massage did better on several measures, including pain and neck movement, at the end of the program and at one month.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35576853/\">[15]</a> In 46 adults with upper-shoulder pain, court-type Thai massage and a Thai hermit exercise routine both improved neck movement and pain, and the massage group reported a clearer reduction in pain.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34293959/\">[16]</a> A pilot trial of 20 people with shoulder-blade pain found lower pain and muscle tension after Thai massage than after a physical-therapy package of ultrasound and a hot pack.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22196428/\">[17]</a></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"style\":{\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"400\"}}} -->\n<p style=\"font-weight:400\">In 62 young adults with tender spots in the forearm muscles, nine short sessions of Thai massage were followed by greater improvement in pain, tenderness and wrist movement than sham ultrasound.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39593435/\">[18]</a></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"style\":{\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"400\"}}} -->\n<p style=\"font-weight:400\">Not every comparison favored Thai massage. In 60 people with heel pain, both Thai massage and ultrasound were followed by less pain, but ultrasound with stretching produced larger gains in pain and ankle movement.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39905464/\">[19]</a> In 48 adults with a forward head posture, a four-week program that combined Thai massage with shoulder-blade exercises and chest mobilization improved head position and neck movement more than lying at rest. Because three approaches were combined, the trial cannot show what Thai massage added by itself.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37605282/\">[20]</a></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n</details>\n<!-- /wp:details -->\n\n<!-- wp:details {\"style\":{\"border\":{\"width\":\"1px\",\"style\":\"solid\",\"color\":\"var(\\u002d\\u002dts-rule)\",\"radius\":\"4px\"},\"color\":{\"background\":\"var(\\u002d\\u002dts-panel)\"},\"spacing\":{\"padding\":{\"top\":\"0.85rem\",\"right\":\"1.1rem\",\"bottom\":\"0.85rem\",\"left\":\"1.1rem\"},\"margin\":{\"bottom\":\"0.7rem\"}},\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"600\"}}} -->\n<details class=\"wp-block-details has-border-color has-background\" style=\"border-color:var(--ts-rule);border-width:1px;border-style:solid;border-radius:4px;background-color:var(--ts-panel);padding-top:0.85rem;padding-right:1.1rem;padding-bottom:0.85rem;padding-left:1.1rem;margin-bottom:0.7rem;font-weight:600\"><summary>Stress, Energy and Recovery</summary>\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"style\":{\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"400\"}}} -->\n<p style=\"font-weight:400\">Two small trials in healthy adults first raised stress with a mental-arithmetic test and then gave either one hour of Thai massage or one hour of rest. In both trials, stress measures fell after massage and after rest. In one trial (29 people, each trying both), one marker, salivary amylase, fell more after Thai massage; in the other (30 people), no measure differed between the groups.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26436433/\">[21]</a><a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26387408/\">[22]</a></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"style\":{\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"400\"}}} -->\n<p style=\"font-weight:400\">In 36 physiotherapy students under academic stress, saliva cortisol and heart rate fell after one Thai massage session and in a control group, with no significant difference in cortisol between the groups.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27210845/\">[23]</a></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"style\":{\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"400\"}}} -->\n<p style=\"font-weight:400\">In a small crossover trial, 20 adults who felt tired or low on energy had three weekly sessions of Thai massage and three of Swedish massage. Both were reported to support well-being, with Thai massage described as more energizing and Swedish massage as having the larger effect on relaxation and sleep.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30100318/\">[24]</a></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"style\":{\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"400\"}}} -->\n<p style=\"font-weight:400\">In 54 healthy adults whose calf muscles were tired by heel-raise exercise, 30 minutes of Thai massage was followed by faster recovery on muscle measures than 30 minutes of mostly rest.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38796061/\">[25]</a></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n</details>\n<!-- /wp:details -->\n\n<!-- wp:details {\"style\":{\"border\":{\"width\":\"1px\",\"style\":\"solid\",\"color\":\"var(\\u002d\\u002dts-rule)\",\"radius\":\"4px\"},\"color\":{\"background\":\"var(\\u002d\\u002dts-panel)\"},\"spacing\":{\"padding\":{\"top\":\"0.85rem\",\"right\":\"1.1rem\",\"bottom\":\"0.85rem\",\"left\":\"1.1rem\"},\"margin\":{\"bottom\":\"0.7rem\"}},\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"600\"}}} -->\n<details class=\"wp-block-details has-border-color has-background\" style=\"border-color:var(--ts-rule);border-width:1px;border-style:solid;border-radius:4px;background-color:var(--ts-panel);padding-top:0.85rem;padding-right:1.1rem;padding-bottom:0.85rem;padding-left:1.1rem;margin-bottom:0.7rem;font-weight:600\"><summary>Sources</summary>\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"style\":{\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"400\"}}} -->\n<p style=\"font-weight:400\">Each number above links to the source. Titles are quoted as published.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list {\"ordered\":true,\"style\":{\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"400\"}}} -->\n<ol class=\"wp-block-list\" style=\"font-weight:400\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">UNESCO Intangible Cultural Heritage. <a href=\"https://ich.unesco.org/en/RL/nuad-thai-traditional-thai-massage-01384\">Nuad Thai, traditional Thai massage</a>. Representative List of the Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity, inscribed 2019.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Keeratitanont K, Jensen MP, Chatchawan U, Auvichayapat P. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25682523/\">The efficacy of traditional Thai massage for the treatment of chronic pain: A systematic review</a>. Complement Ther Clin Pract, 2015.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Netchanok S, Wendy M, Marie C, Siobhan O. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23059437/\">The effectiveness of Swedish massage and traditional Thai massage in treating chronic low back pain: a review of the literature</a>. Complement Ther Clin Pract, 2012.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Furlan AD, Giraldo M, Baskwill A, Irvin E, Imamura M. <a href=\"https://www.cochrane.org/evidence/CD001929_massage-low-back-pain\">Massage for low-back pain</a>. Cochrane Database of Systematic Reviews, 2015, issue 9, CD001929.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Cochrane. <a href=\"https://www.cochrane.org/evidence/CD004871_massage-neck-pain\">Massage for neck pain</a>. Cochrane evidence summary, 2024, CD004871 (searches to October 2023).</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">National Center for Complementary and Integrative Health (NIH). <a href=\"https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/massage-therapy-what-you-need-to-know\">Massage Therapy: What You Need To Know</a>. Last updated May 2019.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Juntakarn C, Prasartritha T, Petrakard P. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28690703/\">The Effectiveness of Thai Massage and Joint Mobilization</a>. Int J Ther Massage Bodywork, 2017.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Laosee O, Sritoomma N, Wamontree P, Rattanapan C, Sitthi-Amorn C. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31987237/\">The effectiveness of traditional Thai massage versus massage with herbal compress among elderly patients with low back pain: A randomised controlled trial</a>. Complement Ther Med, 2020.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Siriyong T, Mangkang J, Yotmadee P. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42142427/\">Clinical efficacy of court-type traditional Thai massage and acupuncture for low back pain management: a randomized controlled trial</a>. Explore (NY), 2026.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Chatchawan U, Thinkhamrop B, Kharmwan S, Knowles J, Eungpinichpong W. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jbmt.2005.02.001\">Effectiveness of traditional Thai massage versus Swedish massage among patients with back pain associated with myofascial trigger points</a>. J Bodyw Mov Ther, 2005.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Kumnerddee W. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21299184/\">Effectiveness comparison between Thai traditional massage and Chinese acupuncture for myofascial back pain in Thai military personnel: a preliminary report</a>. J Med Assoc Thai, 2009.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Buttagat V, Narktro T, Onsrira K, Pobsamai C. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27670864/\">Short-term effects of traditional Thai massage on electromyogram, muscle tension and pain among patients with upper back pain associated with myofascial trigger points</a>. Complement Ther Med, 2016.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Buttagat V, Eungpinichpong W, Chatchawan U, Kharmwan S. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21147414/\">The immediate effects of traditional Thai massage on heart rate variability and stress-related parameters in patients with back pain associated with myofascial trigger points</a>. J Bodyw Mov Ther, 2011.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Buttagat V, Muenpan K, Wiriyasakunphan W, Pomsuwan S, Kluayhomthong S, Areeudomwong P. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34391301/\">A comparative study of Thai massage and muscle energy technique for chronic neck pain: A single-blinded randomized clinical trial</a>. J Bodyw Mov Ther, 2021.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Areeudomwong P, Nakrit R, Seephung T, Ketsawad A, Buttagat V. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35576853/\">A randomized comparative study of traditional Thai massage and Thai boxing exercise on clinical-based outcomes in patients with scapulocostal syndrome</a>. Complement Ther Clin Pract, 2022.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Boonruab J, Poonsuk P, Damjuti W, Supamethangkura W. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34293959/\">Myofascial Pain Syndrome Focused on the Upper Trapezius Muscle: A Comparative Randomized Controlled Trial of the Court-Type Traditional Thai Massage versus the Thai Hermit</a>. J Evid Based Integr Med, 2021.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Buttagat V, Eungpinichpong W, Chatchawan U, Arayawichanon P. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22196428/\">Therapeutic effects of traditional Thai massage on pain, muscle tension and anxiety in patients with scapulocostal syndrome: a randomized single-blinded pilot study</a>. J Bodyw Mov Ther, 2012.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Buttagat V, Kluayhomthong S, Areeudomwong P. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39593435/\">The beneficial effects of traditional Thai massage on young patients with latent myofascial trigger points in the wrist extensor muscles: A randomized controlled trial</a>. J Bodyw Mov Ther, 2024.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Somphai S, Sucharit W, Peungsuwan P, Roberts N, Chatchawan U. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39905464/\">Randomised control trial to compare the efficacy of traditional Thai massage and ultrasound therapy for treating plantar heel pain</a>. BMC Complement Med Ther, 2025.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Buttagat V, Kluayhomthong S, Areeudomwong P. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37605282/\">Effects of the combination of traditional Thai massage, scapular stabilization exercise, and chest mobilization in subjects with forward head posture: a single-blinded randomized clinical trial</a>. Chiropr Man Therap, 2023.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Sripongngam T, Eungpinichpong W, Sirivongs D, Kanpittaya J, Tangvoraphonkchai K, Chanaboon S. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26436433/\">Immediate Effects of Traditional Thai Massage on Psychological Stress as Indicated by Salivary Alpha-Amylase Levels in Healthy Persons</a>. Med Sci Monit Basic Res, 2015.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Sripongngam T, Eungpinichpong W, et al. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26387408/\">Psychological Stress Can Be Decreased by Traditional Thai Massage</a>. J Med Assoc Thai, 2015.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Bennett S, Bennett MJ, Chatchawan U, et al. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27210845/\">Acute effects of traditional Thai massage on cortisol levels, arterial blood pressure and stress perception in academic stress condition: A single blind randomised controlled trial</a>. J Bodyw Mov Ther, 2016.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">MacSween A, Lorrimer S, van Schaik P, Holmes M, van Wersch A. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30100318/\">A randomised crossover trial comparing Thai and Swedish massage for fatigue and depleted energy</a>. J Bodyw Mov Ther, 2018.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Buttagat V, Kluayhomthong S, Areeudomwong P. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38796061/\">The influence of traditional Thai massage on recovery from gastrocnemius muscle fatigue: A single-blind randomised controlled trial</a>. Complement Ther Med, 2024.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ol>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n</details>\n<!-- /wp:details -->\n\n",
   "new_research": "<!-- wp:uagb/advanced-heading {\"block_id\":\"7e4a91c2\",\"classMigrate\":true,\"headingDescToggle\":false,\"headingDescPosition\":\"above-heading\",\"headingAlign\":\"center\",\"separatorColor\":\"var(--ast-global-color-0)\",\"seperatorStyle\":\"solid\",\"seperatorPosition\":\"above-heading\",\"separatorWidth\":64,\"separatorWidthType\":\"px\",\"headSpace\":0,\"separatorSpace\":14,\"blockTopPadding\":100,\"blockRightPadding\":220,\"blockLeftPadding\":220,\"blockBottomPadding\":28,\"blockTopPaddingTablet\":80,\"blockRightPaddingTablet\":40,\"blockLeftPaddingTablet\":40,\"blockBottomPaddingTablet\":24,\"blockTopPaddingMobile\":60,\"blockRightPaddingMobile\":0,\"blockLeftPaddingMobile\":0,\"blockBottomPaddingMobile\":20} -->\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-advanced-heading uagb-block-7e4a91c2\"><div class=\"uagb-separator\"></div><h2 class=\"uagb-heading-text\">What the Research Says About Thai Massage</h2></div>\n<!-- /wp:uagb/advanced-heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Research on traditional Thai massage is still limited. Most studies are small, many compare Thai massage with another approach rather than with no massage, and most follow people for only a few weeks. The summaries below describe what the studies reported. They are general information, not medical advice or a promise of any result, and people respond differently. This section covers studies of massage for pain, movement, stress, energy and recovery; studies of massage used for specific medical conditions are not summarized here. Each part starts with what the studies found in massage’s favor, then gives the results that were weaker, mixed or negative, and ends on what is encouraging: I included the studies I found with disappointing results, not only the favorable ones.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:details {\"showContent\":true,\"style\":{\"border\":{\"width\":\"1px\",\"style\":\"solid\",\"color\":\"var(\\u002d\\u002dts-rule)\",\"radius\":\"4px\"},\"color\":{\"background\":\"var(\\u002d\\u002dts-panel)\"},\"spacing\":{\"padding\":{\"top\":\"0.85rem\",\"right\":\"1.1rem\",\"bottom\":\"0.85rem\",\"left\":\"1.1rem\"},\"margin\":{\"bottom\":\"0.7rem\"}},\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"600\"}}} -->\n<details class=\"wp-block-details has-border-color has-background\" style=\"border-color:var(--ts-rule);border-width:1px;border-style:solid;border-radius:4px;background-color:var(--ts-panel);padding-top:0.85rem;padding-right:1.1rem;padding-bottom:0.85rem;padding-left:1.1rem;margin-bottom:0.7rem;font-weight:600\" open><summary>Reviews, and What They Say About Limits</summary>\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"style\":{\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"400\"}}} -->\n<p style=\"font-weight:400\">A 2015 systematic review of six studies of traditional Thai massage in people with long-lasting pain found that every study reported less pain after a course of sessions, with the reduction appearing to last for up to 15 weeks.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25682523/\">[2]</a> A 2012 review of six studies of Swedish and Thai massage for low-back pain reported that the two appeared to be about equally helpful.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23059437/\">[3]</a></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"style\":{\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"400\"}}} -->\n<p style=\"font-weight:400\">The reviews also say what they could not show. The 2015 review’s authors said more research is needed to learn how Thai massage compares with control conditions over the longer term, and the 2012 review noted that weaknesses in the studies mean more direct comparisons are needed. Reviews of massage in general are cautious, too. A 2015 Cochrane review of 25 trials with 3,096 people with low-back pain found some short-term benefits, but the studies were small and of low quality, and its authors said they had “very little confidence” in the results.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26329399/\">[4]</a> A 2024 Cochrane review of 33 studies with 1,994 people with neck pain found that massage probably made little or no difference compared with a placebo massage after about 12 weeks (low-certainty evidence).<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38415786/\">[5]</a></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"style\":{\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"400\"}}} -->\n<p style=\"font-weight:400\">That neck review also found that higher doses, meaning eight or more sessions of at least 30 minutes over four weeks, favored massage in a subgroup analysis, which suggests that regular sessions may matter.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38415786/\">[5]</a></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"style\":{\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"400\"}}} -->\n<p style=\"font-weight:400\">Massage is generally described as low risk, and where serious problems on record can be traced to who gave the massage, they are mostly tied to people who were not trained massage therapists. The U.S. National Center for Complementary and Integrative Health notes that the risk of harm appears to be low, although rare serious side effects such as a blood clot, nerve injury or bone fracture have been reported.<a href=\"https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/massage-therapy-what-you-need-to-know\">[6]</a> A 2014 review of 40 published reports found 138 adverse events over 11 years, mainly disc herniation, soft-tissue injury, nerve problems, spinal cord injury and tears in the neck arteries, with spinal manipulation inside a massage repeatedly linked to the serious ones. Massage therapists were the practitioners in only 8 of the 138 events (5.8 percent) and chiropractors in 33 (23.9 percent); the rest (70.3 percent) involved unregistered people or other health professionals, and the authors concluded that massage by nonprofessionals and forceful techniques is often associated with adverse events.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25197310/\">[7]</a> A 2003 review of 16 case reports and four case series likewise found that most adverse effects followed exotic techniques or massage by laypeople, with massage therapists rarely implicated, that serious effects were mostly tied to techniques other than Swedish massage, and concluded that serious events are probably true rarities.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12777645/\">[8]</a> For Thai massage specifically, a 2019 case report described a 20-year-old man who developed bleeding around the spinal cord (a spinal epidural hematoma) with delayed leg weakness three days after a Thai massage given by a layperson, a friend, and who needed emergency surgery.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30691861/\">[9]</a> A 2015 commentary also discussed a patient who had a heart attack around the time of a Thai massage and the possible adverse effects of Thai massage.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26865746/\">[10]</a> The U.S. center also advises against using massage to put off seeing a health care provider about a medical problem. Taken together, in the reports that say who gave the massage, trained massage therapists were rarely involved: the serious injuries were mostly linked to people who were not massage therapists, including laypeople and unregistered practitioners, and to forceful or unusual techniques. Serious events are rare: I ask about your health, medications and recent injuries before each session, and I set the pressure and the range of each stretch by what is comfortable for you.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n</details>\n<!-- /wp:details -->\n\n<!-- wp:details {\"style\":{\"border\":{\"width\":\"1px\",\"style\":\"solid\",\"color\":\"var(\\u002d\\u002dts-rule)\",\"radius\":\"4px\"},\"color\":{\"background\":\"var(\\u002d\\u002dts-panel)\"},\"spacing\":{\"padding\":{\"top\":\"0.85rem\",\"right\":\"1.1rem\",\"bottom\":\"0.85rem\",\"left\":\"1.1rem\"},\"margin\":{\"bottom\":\"0.7rem\"}},\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"600\"}}} -->\n<details class=\"wp-block-details has-border-color has-background\" style=\"border-color:var(--ts-rule);border-width:1px;border-style:solid;border-radius:4px;background-color:var(--ts-panel);padding-top:0.85rem;padding-right:1.1rem;padding-bottom:0.85rem;padding-left:1.1rem;margin-bottom:0.7rem;font-weight:600\"><summary>Back Pain</summary>\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"style\":{\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"400\"}}} -->\n<p style=\"font-weight:400\">Two trials with a comparison that was not another massage reported clear differences. In 50 adults with upper-back pain, one 30-minute session of Thai massage was followed by larger reductions in pain, muscle tension and muscle activity than a sham device.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27670864/\">[11]</a> In 36 adults with back pain, one 30-minute session was followed by lower self-reported pain, anxiety and muscle tension and better flexibility than resting on a bed.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21147414/\">[12]</a></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"style\":{\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"400\"}}} -->\n<p style=\"font-weight:400\">Trials that compared Thai massage with another approach found similar results rather than a winner. In a hospital trial in Bangkok, 120 adults had Thai massage or joint mobilization twice a week for four weeks, and both groups improved by similar amounts on pain and everyday-activity scores. The trial had no comparison group that received neither, so it cannot show how much of the change was due to the sessions themselves.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28690703/\">[13]</a> In 140 older adults, Thai massage alone and Thai massage with an herbal compress led to similar improvements over 15 weeks, so the compress added no clear benefit.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31987237/\">[14]</a> In another trial of 140 adults, adding acupuncture to court-type Thai massage made no clear difference after one month, and both groups reported less pain and more movement.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42142427/\">[15]</a> A preliminary trial of 18 military personnel with back pain found less pain after both Thai massage and acupuncture, with one pain-questionnaire score lower after acupuncture.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21299184/\">[16]</a></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"style\":{\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"400\"}}} -->\n<p style=\"font-weight:400\">An earlier trial that compared Thai and Swedish massage for back pain with tender spots in the muscles found that pain fell by more than half in both groups after three weeks and stayed lower for up to a month, with no significant difference between them.<a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jbmt.2005.02.001\">[17]</a></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n</details>\n<!-- /wp:details -->\n\n<!-- wp:details {\"style\":{\"border\":{\"width\":\"1px\",\"style\":\"solid\",\"color\":\"var(\\u002d\\u002dts-rule)\",\"radius\":\"4px\"},\"color\":{\"background\":\"var(\\u002d\\u002dts-panel)\"},\"spacing\":{\"padding\":{\"top\":\"0.85rem\",\"right\":\"1.1rem\",\"bottom\":\"0.85rem\",\"left\":\"1.1rem\"},\"margin\":{\"bottom\":\"0.7rem\"}},\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"600\"}}} -->\n<details class=\"wp-block-details has-border-color has-background\" style=\"border-color:var(--ts-rule);border-width:1px;border-style:solid;border-radius:4px;background-color:var(--ts-panel);padding-top:0.85rem;padding-right:1.1rem;padding-bottom:0.85rem;padding-left:1.1rem;margin-bottom:0.7rem;font-weight:600\"><summary>Neck, Shoulders, Arms and Feet</summary>\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"style\":{\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"400\"}}} -->\n<p style=\"font-weight:400\">In 45 adults with neck pain, eight sessions of Thai massage or of a muscle energy technique over two weeks were each followed by more improvement in pain and neck movement than in a control group.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34391301/\">[18]</a> In 78 adults with shoulder-blade pain, four weeks of Thai massage or Thai boxing exercise both reduced pain, and Thai massage did better on several measures, including pain and neck movement, at the end of the program and at one month.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35576853/\">[19]</a> In 46 adults with upper-shoulder pain, court-type Thai massage and a Thai hermit exercise routine both improved neck movement and pain, and the massage group reported a clearer reduction in pain.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34293959/\">[20]</a> In 62 young adults with tender spots in the forearm muscles, nine short sessions of Thai massage were followed by greater improvement in pain, tenderness and wrist movement than sham ultrasound.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39593435/\">[21]</a></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"style\":{\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"400\"}}} -->\n<p style=\"font-weight:400\">Not every comparison favored Thai massage. In the neck trial there was no clear difference between Thai massage and the muscle energy technique. In 60 people with heel pain, both Thai massage and ultrasound were followed by less pain, but ultrasound with stretching produced larger gains in pain and ankle movement.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39905464/\">[22]</a> In 48 adults with a forward head posture, a four-week program that combined Thai massage with shoulder-blade exercises and chest mobilization improved head position and neck movement more than lying at rest. Because three approaches were combined, the trial cannot show what Thai massage added by itself.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37605282/\">[23]</a> In 30 healthy female office workers who each tried three styles of Thai massage, the authors reported that “strength in shoulders was not significantly different, but some forearm fatigue was decreased significantly among the three massage techniques.”<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25892379/\">[24]</a></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"style\":{\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"400\"}}} -->\n<p style=\"font-weight:400\">A pilot trial of 20 people with shoulder-blade pain found lower pain and muscle tension after Thai massage than after a physical-therapy package of ultrasound and a hot pack.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22196428/\">[25]</a></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n</details>\n<!-- /wp:details -->\n\n<!-- wp:details {\"style\":{\"border\":{\"width\":\"1px\",\"style\":\"solid\",\"color\":\"var(\\u002d\\u002dts-rule)\",\"radius\":\"4px\"},\"color\":{\"background\":\"var(\\u002d\\u002dts-panel)\"},\"spacing\":{\"padding\":{\"top\":\"0.85rem\",\"right\":\"1.1rem\",\"bottom\":\"0.85rem\",\"left\":\"1.1rem\"},\"margin\":{\"bottom\":\"0.7rem\"}},\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"600\"}}} -->\n<details class=\"wp-block-details has-border-color has-background\" style=\"border-color:var(--ts-rule);border-width:1px;border-style:solid;border-radius:4px;background-color:var(--ts-panel);padding-top:0.85rem;padding-right:1.1rem;padding-bottom:0.85rem;padding-left:1.1rem;margin-bottom:0.7rem;font-weight:600\"><summary>Stress, Energy and Recovery</summary>\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"style\":{\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"400\"}}} -->\n<p style=\"font-weight:400\">In 54 healthy adults whose calf muscles were tired by heel-raise exercise, 30 minutes of Thai massage was followed by faster recovery on muscle measures than 30 minutes of mostly rest.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38796061/\">[26]</a> In 34 soccer players who were given Thai massage or rest, all seven fitness tests (sit-and-reach, hand grip strength, agility, a 50-meter sprint, sit-ups, push-ups and a test of aerobic fitness) improved in the massage group, compared with two in the rest group.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25729203/\">[27]</a></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"style\":{\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"400\"}}} -->\n<p style=\"font-weight:400\">The results on stress are mixed. Two small trials in healthy adults first raised stress with a mental-arithmetic test and then gave either one hour of Thai massage or one hour of rest. In both trials, stress measures fell after massage and after rest. In one trial (29 people, each trying both), one marker, salivary amylase, fell more after Thai massage; in the other (30 people), no measure differed between the groups.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26436433/\">[28]</a><a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26387408/\">[29]</a> In 36 physiotherapy students under academic stress, saliva cortisol and heart rate fell after one Thai massage session and in a control group, with no significant difference in cortisol between the groups.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27210845/\">[30]</a> In a small crossover trial, 20 adults who felt tired or low on energy had three weekly sessions of Thai massage and three of Swedish massage, and Swedish massage was described as having the larger effect on relaxation and sleep.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30100318/\">[31]</a></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"style\":{\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"400\"}}} -->\n<p style=\"font-weight:400\">Even so, the results point the same encouraging way on the outcomes that matter to many people: a marker of stress, salivary amylase, fell more after Thai massage than after rest in one trial,<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26436433/\">[28]</a> and in the trial of tired adults Thai massage was described as the more energizing of the two styles.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30100318/\">[31]</a></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n</details>\n<!-- /wp:details -->\n\n<!-- wp:details {\"style\":{\"border\":{\"width\":\"1px\",\"style\":\"solid\",\"color\":\"var(\\u002d\\u002dts-rule)\",\"radius\":\"4px\"},\"color\":{\"background\":\"var(\\u002d\\u002dts-panel)\"},\"spacing\":{\"padding\":{\"top\":\"0.85rem\",\"right\":\"1.1rem\",\"bottom\":\"0.85rem\",\"left\":\"1.1rem\"},\"margin\":{\"bottom\":\"0.7rem\"}},\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"600\"}}} -->\n<details class=\"wp-block-details has-border-color has-background\" style=\"border-color:var(--ts-rule);border-width:1px;border-style:solid;border-radius:4px;background-color:var(--ts-panel);padding-top:0.85rem;padding-right:1.1rem;padding-bottom:0.85rem;padding-left:1.1rem;margin-bottom:0.7rem;font-weight:600\"><summary>Sources</summary>\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"style\":{\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"400\"}}} -->\n<p style=\"font-weight:400\">Each number above links to the source. Titles are quoted as published.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list {\"ordered\":true,\"style\":{\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"400\"}}} -->\n<ol class=\"wp-block-list\" style=\"font-weight:400\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">UNESCO Intangible Cultural Heritage. <a href=\"https://ich.unesco.org/en/RL/nuad-thai-traditional-thai-massage-01384\">Nuad Thai, traditional Thai massage</a>. Representative List of the Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity, inscribed 2019.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Keeratitanont K, Jensen MP, Chatchawan U, Auvichayapat P. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25682523/\">The efficacy of traditional Thai massage for the treatment of chronic pain: A systematic review</a>. Complement Ther Clin Pract, 2015.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Netchanok S, Wendy M, Marie C, Siobhan O. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23059437/\">The effectiveness of Swedish massage and traditional Thai massage in treating chronic low back pain: a review of the literature</a>. Complement Ther Clin Pract, 2012.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Furlan AD, Giraldo M, Baskwill A, Irvin E, Imamura M. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26329399/\">Massage for low-back pain</a>. Cochrane Database Syst Rev, 2015.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Gross AR, Lee H, Ezzo J, Chacko N, Gelley G, Forget M, Morien A, Graham N, Santaguida PL, Rice M, Dixon C. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38415786/\">Massage for neck pain</a>. Cochrane Database Syst Rev, 2024 (searches to October 2023).</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">National Center for Complementary and Integrative Health (NIH). <a href=\"https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/massage-therapy-what-you-need-to-know\">Massage Therapy: What You Need To Know</a>. Last updated May 2019.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Yin P, Gao N, Wu J, Litscher G, Xu S. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25197310/\">Adverse events of massage therapy in pain-related conditions: a systematic review</a>. Evid Based Complement Alternat Med, 2014.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Ernst E. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12777645/\">The safety of massage therapy</a>. Rheumatology (Oxford), 2003.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Vanichkulbodee A, Issaragrisil S, Inboriboon PC. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30691861/\">Massage-induced spinal epidural hematoma presenting with delayed paraplegia</a>. Am J Emerg Med, 2019.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Wiwanitkit V. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26865746/\">Thai traditional massage: Issues causing possible adverse effects</a>. Anc Sci Life, 2015.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Buttagat V, Narktro T, Onsrira K, Pobsamai C. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27670864/\">Short-term effects of traditional Thai massage on electromyogram, muscle tension and pain among patients with upper back pain associated with myofascial trigger points</a>. Complement Ther Med, 2016.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Buttagat V, Eungpinichpong W, Chatchawan U, Kharmwan S. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21147414/\">The immediate effects of traditional Thai massage on heart rate variability and stress-related parameters in patients with back pain associated with myofascial trigger points</a>. J Bodyw Mov Ther, 2011.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Juntakarn C, Prasartritha T, Petrakard P. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28690703/\">The Effectiveness of Thai Massage and Joint Mobilization</a>. Int J Ther Massage Bodywork, 2017.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Laosee O, Sritoomma N, Wamontree P, Rattanapan C, Sitthi-Amorn C. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31987237/\">The effectiveness of traditional Thai massage versus massage with herbal compress among elderly patients with low back pain: A randomised controlled trial</a>. Complement Ther Med, 2020.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Siriyong T, Mangkang J, Yotmadee P. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42142427/\">Clinical efficacy of court-type traditional Thai massage and acupuncture for low back pain management: a randomized controlled trial</a>. Explore (NY), 2026.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Kumnerddee W. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21299184/\">Effectiveness comparison between Thai traditional massage and Chinese acupuncture for myofascial back pain in Thai military personnel: a preliminary report</a>. J Med Assoc Thai, 2009.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Chatchawan U, Thinkhamrop B, Kharmwan S, Knowles J, Eungpinichpong W. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jbmt.2005.02.001\">Effectiveness of traditional Thai massage versus Swedish massage among patients with back pain associated with myofascial trigger points</a>. J Bodyw Mov Ther, 2005.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Buttagat V, Muenpan K, Wiriyasakunphan W, Pomsuwan S, Kluayhomthong S, Areeudomwong P. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34391301/\">A comparative study of Thai massage and muscle energy technique for chronic neck pain: A single-blinded randomized clinical trial</a>. J Bodyw Mov Ther, 2021.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Areeudomwong P, Nakrit R, Seephung T, Ketsawad A, Buttagat V. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35576853/\">A randomized comparative study of traditional Thai massage and Thai boxing exercise on clinical-based outcomes in patients with scapulocostal syndrome</a>. Complement Ther Clin Pract, 2022.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Boonruab J, Poonsuk P, Damjuti W, Supamethangkura W. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34293959/\">Myofascial Pain Syndrome Focused on the Upper Trapezius Muscle: A Comparative Randomized Controlled Trial of the Court-Type Traditional Thai Massage versus the Thai Hermit</a>. J Evid Based Integr Med, 2021.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Buttagat V, Kluayhomthong S, Areeudomwong P. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39593435/\">The beneficial effects of traditional Thai massage on young patients with latent myofascial trigger points in the wrist extensor muscles: A randomized controlled trial</a>. J Bodyw Mov Ther, 2024.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Somphai S, Sucharit W, Peungsuwan P, Roberts N, Chatchawan U. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39905464/\">Randomised control trial to compare the efficacy of traditional Thai massage and ultrasound therapy for treating plantar heel pain</a>. BMC Complement Med Ther, 2025.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Buttagat V, Kluayhomthong S, Areeudomwong P. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37605282/\">Effects of the combination of traditional Thai massage, scapular stabilization exercise, and chest mobilization in subjects with forward head posture: a single-blinded randomized clinical trial</a>. Chiropr Man Therap, 2023.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Yoopat P, Maes C, Poriau S, Vanwonterghem K. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25892379/\">Thai traditional massage: efficiency-assessment of three traditional massage methods on office workers: an explorative study</a>. J Bodyw Mov Ther, 2015.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Buttagat V, Eungpinichpong W, Chatchawan U, Arayawichanon P. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22196428/\">Therapeutic effects of traditional Thai massage on pain, muscle tension and anxiety in patients with scapulocostal syndrome: a randomized single-blinded pilot study</a>. J Bodyw Mov Ther, 2012.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Buttagat V, Kluayhomthong S, Areeudomwong P. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38796061/\">The influence of traditional Thai massage on recovery from gastrocnemius muscle fatigue: A single-blind randomised controlled trial</a>. Complement Ther Med, 2024.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Hongsuwan C, Eungpinichpong W, Chatchawan U, Yamauchi J. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25729203/\">Effects of Thai massage on physical fitness in soccer players</a>. J Phys Ther Sci, 2015.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Sripongngam T, Eungpinichpong W, Sirivongs D, Kanpittaya J, Tangvoraphonkchai K, Chanaboon S. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26436433/\">Immediate Effects of Traditional Thai Massage on Psychological Stress as Indicated by Salivary Alpha-Amylase Levels in Healthy Persons</a>. Med Sci Monit Basic Res, 2015.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Sripongngam T, Eungpinichpong W, Sirivongs D, Kanpittaya J, Tangvoraphonkchai K, Chanaboon S. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26387408/\">Psychological Stress Can Be Decreased by Traditional Thai Massage</a>. J Med Assoc Thai, 2015.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Bennett S, Bennett MJ, Chatchawan U, Jenjaiwit P, Pantumethakul R, Kunhasura S, Eungpinichpong W. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27210845/\">Acute effects of traditional Thai massage on cortisol levels, arterial blood pressure and stress perception in academic stress condition: A single blind randomised controlled trial</a>. J Bodyw Mov Ther, 2016.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">MacSween A, Lorrimer S, van Schaik P, Holmes M, van Wersch A. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30100318/\">A randomised crossover trial comparing Thai and Swedish massage for fatigue and depleted energy</a>. J Bodyw Mov Ther, 2018.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ol>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n</details>\n<!-- /wp:details -->\n\n",
   "research_start": "<!-- wp:uagb/advanced-heading {\"block_id\":\"7e4a91c2\",\"classMigrate\":true,\"headingDescToggle\":false,\"headingDescPosition\":\"above-heading\",\"headingAlign\":\"center\",\"separatorColor\":\"var(--ast-global-color-0)\",\"seperatorStyle\":\"solid\",\"seperatorPosition\":\"above-heading\",\"separatorWidth\":64,\"separatorWidthType\":\"px\",\"headSpace\":0,\"separatorSpace\":14,\"blockTopPadding\":100,\"blockRightPadding\":220,\"blockLeftPadding\":220,\"blockBottomPadding\":28,\"blockTopPaddingTablet\":80,\"blockRightPaddingTablet\":40,\"blockLeftPaddingTablet\":40,\"blockBottomPaddingTablet\":24,\"blockTopPaddingMobile\":60,\"blockRightPaddingMobile\":0,\"blockLeftPaddingMobile\":0,\"blockBottomPaddingMobile\":20} -->",
   "source_count": 31,
   "heading": "What the Research Says About Thai Massage"
  },
  {
   "slug": "therapeutic-massage",
   "label": "Therapeutic Massage",
   "old_research": "<!-- wp:uagb/advanced-heading {\"block_id\":\"6c2e9b47\",\"classMigrate\":true,\"headingDescToggle\":false,\"headingDescPosition\":\"above-heading\",\"headingAlign\":\"center\",\"separatorColor\":\"var(--ast-global-color-0)\",\"seperatorStyle\":\"solid\",\"seperatorPosition\":\"above-heading\",\"separatorWidth\":64,\"separatorWidthType\":\"px\",\"headSpace\":0,\"separatorSpace\":14,\"blockTopPadding\":100,\"blockRightPadding\":220,\"blockLeftPadding\":220,\"blockBottomPadding\":28,\"blockTopPaddingTablet\":80,\"blockRightPaddingTablet\":40,\"blockLeftPaddingTablet\":40,\"blockBottomPaddingTablet\":24,\"blockTopPaddingMobile\":60,\"blockRightPaddingMobile\":0,\"blockLeftPaddingMobile\":0,\"blockBottomPaddingMobile\":20} -->\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-advanced-heading uagb-block-6c2e9b47\"><div class=\"uagb-separator\"></div><h2 class=\"uagb-heading-text\">What the Research Says About Therapeutic Massage</h2></div>\n<!-- /wp:uagb/advanced-heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Research on massage therapy is large but mixed. Many studies are small, results often depend on what massage is compared with, and many follow people for only a few weeks. The summaries below describe what the studies reported. They are general information, not medical advice or a promise of any result, and people respond differently.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:details {\"showContent\":true,\"style\":{\"border\":{\"width\":\"1px\",\"style\":\"solid\",\"color\":\"var(\\u002d\\u002dts-rule)\",\"radius\":\"4px\"},\"color\":{\"background\":\"var(\\u002d\\u002dts-panel)\"},\"spacing\":{\"padding\":{\"top\":\"0.85rem\",\"right\":\"1.1rem\",\"bottom\":\"0.85rem\",\"left\":\"1.1rem\"},\"margin\":{\"bottom\":\"0.7rem\"}},\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"600\"}}} -->\n<details class=\"wp-block-details has-border-color has-background\" style=\"border-color:var(--ts-rule);border-width:1px;border-style:solid;border-radius:4px;background-color:var(--ts-panel);padding-top:0.85rem;padding-right:1.1rem;padding-bottom:0.85rem;padding-left:1.1rem;margin-bottom:0.7rem;font-weight:600\" open><summary>Reviews, and What They Say About Limits</summary>\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"style\":{\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"400\"}}} -->\n<p style=\"font-weight:400\">A 2024 review that mapped 17 systematic reviews of massage for pain found that none of its conclusions was rated high-certainty. Seven were rated moderate-certainty, all favoring massage for pain, and conclusions that massage was better than other active approaches were rare.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39008297/\">[1]</a> An earlier evidence map of 49 systematic reviews found that the high-quality reviews reached conclusions of low strength of evidence, because few large, rigorous studies had been done.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30892910/\">[11]</a></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"style\":{\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"400\"}}} -->\n<p style=\"font-weight:400\">A 2016 meta-analysis of 67 randomized trials found lower pain scores after massage than after sham massage, no massage or other approaches. The authors made a strong recommendation only for the comparison with no massage and a weak one for the others.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27165971/\">[2]</a></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"style\":{\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"400\"}}} -->\n<p style=\"font-weight:400\">A 2015 review of 26 randomized trials with 2,565 people with muscle and joint problems found low-to-moderate-level evidence that massage reduced pain in the short term compared with no intervention in some groups, such as people with shoulder pain, but not in people with neck or low-back pain. Function improved in the short term for people with low-back and shoulder pain. Compared with another active approach, no clear benefit was seen.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26093806/\">[12]</a></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"style\":{\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"400\"}}} -->\n<p style=\"font-weight:400\">The U.S. National Center for Complementary and Integrative Health notes that the risk of harm from massage appears to be low, although rare serious side effects such as a blood clot, nerve injury or bone fracture have been reported, and it advises against using massage to put off seeing a health care provider about a medical problem.<a href=\"https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/massage-therapy-what-you-need-to-know\">[9]</a> A 2003 review of published case reports concluded that massage is not entirely risk free but that serious adverse events are probably true rarities, and most of those reported followed unusual techniques or massage by untrained people rather than by massage therapists.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12777645/\">[13]</a></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n</details>\n<!-- /wp:details -->\n\n<!-- wp:details {\"style\":{\"border\":{\"width\":\"1px\",\"style\":\"solid\",\"color\":\"var(\\u002d\\u002dts-rule)\",\"radius\":\"4px\"},\"color\":{\"background\":\"var(\\u002d\\u002dts-panel)\"},\"spacing\":{\"padding\":{\"top\":\"0.85rem\",\"right\":\"1.1rem\",\"bottom\":\"0.85rem\",\"left\":\"1.1rem\"},\"margin\":{\"bottom\":\"0.7rem\"}},\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"600\"}}} -->\n<details class=\"wp-block-details has-border-color has-background\" style=\"border-color:var(--ts-rule);border-width:1px;border-style:solid;border-radius:4px;background-color:var(--ts-panel);padding-top:0.85rem;padding-right:1.1rem;padding-bottom:0.85rem;padding-left:1.1rem;margin-bottom:0.7rem;font-weight:600\"><summary>Neck, Shoulders and Back</summary>\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"style\":{\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"400\"}}} -->\n<p style=\"font-weight:400\">A 2024 Cochrane review of 33 studies with 1,994 people with neck pain found that, compared with a placebo massage, massage probably makes little or no difference to pain, function or quality of life at up to 12 weeks (low-certainty evidence). A subgroup analysis favored higher doses of eight or more sessions of at least 30 minutes over four weeks, and adverse events were too poorly reported to judge.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38415786/\">[10]</a></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"style\":{\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"400\"}}} -->\n<p style=\"font-weight:400\">A 2015 Cochrane review of 25 trials with 3,096 people with low-back pain found that massage was better than no intervention for pain in the short term but not over the long term, and that the quality of the evidence was low to very low. The authors said they had “very little confidence” in the results. No serious side effects were reported; the most common minor one was more pain, in 1.5 to 25 percent of people.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26329399/\">[14]</a></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"style\":{\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"400\"}}} -->\n<p style=\"font-weight:400\">A 2014 review of 20 trials of soft-tissue massage for shoulder pain found low-quality evidence that massage improved shoulder movement, pain and function compared with no intervention in the short term, right after the sessions ended.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22844035/\">[15]</a> In a trial of 80 adults with shoulder pain who were all doing exercises, adding four weeks of massage gave no extra benefit for pain, disability or movement, and a small difference in pain at 12 weeks favored exercise alone.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26023217/\">[16]</a></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"style\":{\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"400\"}}} -->\n<p style=\"font-weight:400\">In a crossover study of 17 healthy adults, muscle activity in the upper-shoulder muscles fell by 19 percent after a short, moderate-pressure massage of the shoulders and neck, compared with 1 percent after sitting quietly. The study measured muscle activity, not pain.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27977513/\">[17]</a></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"style\":{\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"400\"}}} -->\n<p style=\"font-weight:400\">In a pilot study of 45 cardiac sonographers with work-related muscle discomfort, weekly 30-minute massages for 10 weeks, with or without stretching, were followed by some improvement in discomfort scores compared with no intervention. The authors said larger trials are needed.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20846441/\">[18]</a></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n</details>\n<!-- /wp:details -->\n\n<!-- wp:details {\"style\":{\"border\":{\"width\":\"1px\",\"style\":\"solid\",\"color\":\"var(\\u002d\\u002dts-rule)\",\"radius\":\"4px\"},\"color\":{\"background\":\"var(\\u002d\\u002dts-panel)\"},\"spacing\":{\"padding\":{\"top\":\"0.85rem\",\"right\":\"1.1rem\",\"bottom\":\"0.85rem\",\"left\":\"1.1rem\"},\"margin\":{\"bottom\":\"0.7rem\"}},\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"600\"}}} -->\n<details class=\"wp-block-details has-border-color has-background\" style=\"border-color:var(--ts-rule);border-width:1px;border-style:solid;border-radius:4px;background-color:var(--ts-panel);padding-top:0.85rem;padding-right:1.1rem;padding-bottom:0.85rem;padding-left:1.1rem;margin-bottom:0.7rem;font-weight:600\"><summary>Exercise, Flexibility and Recovery</summary>\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"style\":{\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"400\"}}} -->\n<p style=\"font-weight:400\">A 2020 review of 29 randomized studies with 1,012 people found no evidence that massage improved strength, jumping, sprinting, endurance or fatigue, but it found small improvements in flexibility and in delayed-onset muscle soreness.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32426160/\">[3]</a></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"style\":{\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"400\"}}} -->\n<p style=\"font-weight:400\">A 2016 review of 22 trials of massage after exercise concluded that the effects on performance recovery are rather small and partly unclear. Shorter massage (5 to 12 minutes) tended to show larger effects than longer massage, and the authors questioned whether the limited effects justify using massage as a recovery method in competitive athletes.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26744335/\">[19]</a></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"style\":{\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"400\"}}} -->\n<p style=\"font-weight:400\">In 52 healthy volunteers whose grip had been tired by exercise, five minutes of forearm and hand massage was followed by better grip performance than five minutes of rest or of passive joint movement. The study was small and measured only the minutes right afterward.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16398602/\">[20]</a></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"style\":{\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"400\"}}} -->\n<p style=\"font-weight:400\">In 10 recreationally active women, 30 seconds of massage at the back of the thigh was followed by about a 7 percent larger hip-bend range than no massage, with no change in muscle tension or muscle activity.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20543728/\">[21]</a></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n</details>\n<!-- /wp:details -->\n\n<!-- wp:details {\"style\":{\"border\":{\"width\":\"1px\",\"style\":\"solid\",\"color\":\"var(\\u002d\\u002dts-rule)\",\"radius\":\"4px\"},\"color\":{\"background\":\"var(\\u002d\\u002dts-panel)\"},\"spacing\":{\"padding\":{\"top\":\"0.85rem\",\"right\":\"1.1rem\",\"bottom\":\"0.85rem\",\"left\":\"1.1rem\"},\"margin\":{\"bottom\":\"0.7rem\"}},\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"600\"}}} -->\n<details class=\"wp-block-details has-border-color has-background\" style=\"border-color:var(--ts-rule);border-width:1px;border-style:solid;border-radius:4px;background-color:var(--ts-panel);padding-top:0.85rem;padding-right:1.1rem;padding-bottom:0.85rem;padding-left:1.1rem;margin-bottom:0.7rem;font-weight:600\"><summary>Stress, Relaxation and Well-Being</summary>\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"style\":{\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"400\"}}} -->\n<p style=\"font-weight:400\">A 2004 meta-analysis of 37 randomized studies found that a single massage was followed by lower state anxiety, blood pressure and heart rate, but not by lower negative mood, lower cortisol or less immediate pain.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/14717648/\">[22]</a> A 2011 review of massage and the stress hormone cortisol found that the effect was generally very small and in most analyses not statistically distinguishable from zero.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21147413/\">[23]</a></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"style\":{\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"400\"}}} -->\n<p style=\"font-weight:400\">In 60 healthy women, heart-rate variability and feelings of relaxation rose, and feelings of stress fell, in two massage groups and in a resting group; the two massage groups showed higher heart-rate variability than the resting group.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32901072/\">[4]</a> In 14 healthy men, a 20-minute massage and a 20-minute reading period were each followed by a rise in the hormone oxytocin, which led the researchers to suggest that touch is not needed for that rise.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18576922/\">[24]</a></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"style\":{\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"400\"}}} -->\n<p style=\"font-weight:400\">In 118 healthy adults who first took a stressful laboratory test, a single massage of about an hour was not followed by significantly better well-being scores or cortisol levels than a sham massage, although most people who had the real massage described it as relaxing (82 to 100 percent, compared with 42 percent after the sham).<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26365448/\">[25]</a></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"style\":{\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"400\"}}} -->\n<p style=\"font-weight:400\">In a pilot study of 54 adults aged 60 and over, twice-weekly massage for four weeks was followed by better scores for well-being and perceived stress than guided relaxation.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/17709060/\">[5]</a></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"style\":{\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"400\"}}} -->\n<p style=\"font-weight:400\">In a trial of 122 adults at an integrative-therapy clinic in Brazil, people had eight massage sessions followed by rest, massage followed by Reiki, or no intervention. Stress fell more in the massage-plus-Reiki group than in the no-intervention group, and the authors called for a placebo group in later trials.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27901219/\">[7]</a> The same trial’s report on stress and quality of life (101 people analyzed) found that both massage groups did better than no intervention.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33053005/\">[8]</a></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"style\":{\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"400\"}}} -->\n<p style=\"font-weight:400\">In 38 family caregivers at a rehabilitation hospital who had either one or three massages a week for two weeks, psychological symptom scores were lower afterward in both groups. Because everyone had massage, the study cannot show how much of the change was due to the massage itself.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31003683/\">[6]</a></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n</details>\n<!-- /wp:details -->\n\n<!-- wp:details {\"style\":{\"border\":{\"width\":\"1px\",\"style\":\"solid\",\"color\":\"var(\\u002d\\u002dts-rule)\",\"radius\":\"4px\"},\"color\":{\"background\":\"var(\\u002d\\u002dts-panel)\"},\"spacing\":{\"padding\":{\"top\":\"0.85rem\",\"right\":\"1.1rem\",\"bottom\":\"0.85rem\",\"left\":\"1.1rem\"},\"margin\":{\"bottom\":\"0.7rem\"}},\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"600\"}}} -->\n<details class=\"wp-block-details has-border-color has-background\" style=\"border-color:var(--ts-rule);border-width:1px;border-style:solid;border-radius:4px;background-color:var(--ts-panel);padding-top:0.85rem;padding-right:1.1rem;padding-bottom:0.85rem;padding-left:1.1rem;margin-bottom:0.7rem;font-weight:600\"><summary>Sources</summary>\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"style\":{\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"400\"}}} -->\n<p style=\"font-weight:400\">Each number above links to the source. Titles are quoted as published.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list {\"ordered\":true,\"style\":{\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"400\"}}} -->\n<ol class=\"wp-block-list\" style=\"font-weight:400\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Mak S, Allen J, Begashaw M, Miake-Lye I, Beroes-Severin J, De Vries G, Lawson E, Shekelle PG. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39008297/\">Use of Massage Therapy for Pain, 2018-2023: A Systematic Review</a>. JAMA Netw Open, 2024.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Crawford C, Boyd C, Paat CF, Price A, Xenakis L, Yang E, Zhang W; Evidence for Massage Therapy (EMT) Working Group. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27165971/\">The Impact of Massage Therapy on Function in Pain Populations-A Systematic Review and Meta-Analysis of Randomized Controlled Trials: Part I, Patients Experiencing Pain in the General Population</a>. Pain Med, 2016.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Davis HL, Alabed S, Chico TJA. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32426160/\">Effect of sports massage on performance and recovery: a systematic review and meta-analysis</a>. BMJ Open Sport Exerc Med, 2020.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Meier M, Unternaehrer E, Dimitroff SJ, Benz ABE, Bentele UU, Schorpp SM, Wenzel M, Pruessner JC. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32901072/\">Standardized massage interventions as protocols for the induction of psychophysiological relaxation in the laboratory: a block randomized, controlled trial</a>. Sci Rep, 2020.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Sharpe PA, Williams HG, Granner ML, Hussey JR. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/17709060/\">A randomised study of the effects of massage therapy compared to guided relaxation on well-being and stress perception among older adults</a>. Complement Ther Med, 2007.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Williams NA, Burnfield JM, Springer P, Wolf K, Buster T. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31003683/\">Therapeutic massage to enhance family caregivers' well-being in a rehabilitation hospital</a>. Complement Ther Clin Pract, 2019.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Kurebayashi LF, Turrini RN, Souza TP, Takiguchi RS, Kuba G, Nagumo MT. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27901219/\">Massage and Reiki used to reduce stress and anxiety: Randomized Clinical Trial</a>. Rev Lat Am Enfermagem, 2016.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Kurebayashi LFS, Gnatta JR, Kuba G, Giaponesi ALL, Souza TPB, Turrini RNT. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33053005/\">Massage and Reiki to reduce stress and improve quality of life: a randomized clinical trial</a>. Rev Esc Enferm USP, 2020.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">National Center for Complementary and Integrative Health (NIH). <a href=\"https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/massage-therapy-what-you-need-to-know\">Massage Therapy: What You Need To Know</a>. Last updated May 2019.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Gross AR, Lee H, Ezzo J, Chacko N, Gelley G, Forget M, Morien A, Graham N, Santaguida PL, Rice M, Dixon C. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38415786/\">Massage for neck pain</a>. Cochrane Database Syst Rev, 2024 (searches to October 2023).</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Miake-Lye IM, Mak S, Lee J, Luger T, Taylor SL, Shanman R, Beroes-Severin JM, Shekelle PG. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30892910/\">Massage for Pain: An Evidence Map</a>. J Altern Complement Med, 2019.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Bervoets DC, Luijsterburg PA, Alessie JJ, Buijs MJ, Verhagen AP. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26093806/\">Massage therapy has short-term benefits for people with common musculoskeletal disorders compared to no treatment: a systematic review</a>. J Physiother, 2015.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Ernst E. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12777645/\">The safety of massage therapy</a>. Rheumatology (Oxford), 2003.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Furlan AD, Giraldo M, Baskwill A, Irvin E, Imamura M. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26329399/\">Massage for low-back pain</a>. Cochrane Database Syst Rev, 2015.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">van den Dolder PA, Ferreira PH, Refshauge KM. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22844035/\">Effectiveness of soft tissue massage and exercise for the treatment of non-specific shoulder pain: a systematic review with meta-analysis</a>. Br J Sports Med, 2014.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">van den Dolder PA, Ferreira PH, Refshauge KM. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26023217/\">Effectiveness of Soft Tissue Massage for Nonspecific Shoulder Pain: Randomized Controlled Trial</a>. Phys Ther, 2015.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Domingo AR, Diek M, Goble KM, Maluf KS, Goble DJ, Baweja HS. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27977513/\">Short-duration therapeutic massage reduces postural upper trapezius muscle activity</a>. Neuroreport, 2017.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Engen DJ, Wahner-Roedler DL, Nadolny AM, Persinger CM, Oh JK, Spittell PC, Loehrer LL, Cha SS, Bauer BA. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20846441/\">The effect of chair massage on muscular discomfort in cardiac sonographers: a pilot study</a>. BMC Complement Altern Med, 2010.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Poppendieck W, Wegmann M, Ferrauti A, Kellmann M, Pfeiffer M, Meyer T. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26744335/\">Massage and Performance Recovery: A Meta-Analytical Review</a>. Sports Med, 2016.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Brooks CP, Woodruff LD, Wright LL, Donatelli R. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16398602/\">The immediate effects of manual massage on power-grip performance after maximal exercise in healthy adults</a>. J Altern Complement Med, 2005.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Huang SY, Di Santo M, Wadden KP, Cappa DF, Alkanani T, Behm DG. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20543728/\">Short-duration massage at the hamstrings musculotendinous junction induces greater range of motion</a>. J Strength Cond Res, 2010.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Moyer CA, Rounds J, Hannum JW. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/14717648/\">A meta-analysis of massage therapy research</a>. Psychol Bull, 2004.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Moyer CA, Seefeldt L, Mann ES, Jackley LM. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21147413/\">Does massage therapy reduce cortisol? A comprehensive quantitative review</a>. J Bodyw Mov Ther, 2011.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Bello D, White-Traut R, Schwertz D, Pournajafi-Nazarloo H, Carter CS. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18576922/\">An exploratory study of neurohormonal responses of healthy men to massage</a>. J Altern Complement Med, 2008.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Kanitz JL, Reif M, Rihs C, Krause I, Seifert G. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26365448/\">A randomised, controlled, single-blinded study on the impact of a single rhythmical massage (anthroposophic medicine) on well-being and salivary cortisol in healthy adults</a>. Complement Ther Med, 2015.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ol>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n</details>\n<!-- /wp:details -->\n",
   "new_research": "<!-- wp:uagb/advanced-heading {\"block_id\":\"6c2e9b47\",\"classMigrate\":true,\"headingDescToggle\":false,\"headingDescPosition\":\"above-heading\",\"headingAlign\":\"center\",\"separatorColor\":\"var(--ast-global-color-0)\",\"seperatorStyle\":\"solid\",\"seperatorPosition\":\"above-heading\",\"separatorWidth\":64,\"separatorWidthType\":\"px\",\"headSpace\":0,\"separatorSpace\":14,\"blockTopPadding\":100,\"blockRightPadding\":220,\"blockLeftPadding\":220,\"blockBottomPadding\":28,\"blockTopPaddingTablet\":80,\"blockRightPaddingTablet\":40,\"blockLeftPaddingTablet\":40,\"blockBottomPaddingTablet\":24,\"blockTopPaddingMobile\":60,\"blockRightPaddingMobile\":0,\"blockLeftPaddingMobile\":0,\"blockBottomPaddingMobile\":20} -->\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-advanced-heading uagb-block-6c2e9b47\"><div class=\"uagb-separator\"></div><h2 class=\"uagb-heading-text\">What the Research Says About Therapeutic Massage</h2></div>\n<!-- /wp:uagb/advanced-heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Research on massage therapy is large but mixed. Many studies are small, results often depend on what massage is compared with, and many follow people for only a few weeks. The summaries below describe what the studies reported. They are general information, not medical advice or a promise of any result, and people respond differently. This section covers studies of massage for pain, movement, soreness, stress and well-being; studies of massage used for specific medical conditions are not summarized here. Each part starts with what the studies found in massage’s favor, then gives the results that were weaker, mixed or negative, and ends on what is encouraging: I included the studies I found with disappointing results, not only the favorable ones.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:details {\"showContent\":true,\"style\":{\"border\":{\"width\":\"1px\",\"style\":\"solid\",\"color\":\"var(\\u002d\\u002dts-rule)\",\"radius\":\"4px\"},\"color\":{\"background\":\"var(\\u002d\\u002dts-panel)\"},\"spacing\":{\"padding\":{\"top\":\"0.85rem\",\"right\":\"1.1rem\",\"bottom\":\"0.85rem\",\"left\":\"1.1rem\"},\"margin\":{\"bottom\":\"0.7rem\"}},\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"600\"}}} -->\n<details class=\"wp-block-details has-border-color has-background\" style=\"border-color:var(--ts-rule);border-width:1px;border-style:solid;border-radius:4px;background-color:var(--ts-panel);padding-top:0.85rem;padding-right:1.1rem;padding-bottom:0.85rem;padding-left:1.1rem;margin-bottom:0.7rem;font-weight:600\" open><summary>Reviews, and What They Say About Limits</summary>\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"style\":{\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"400\"}}} -->\n<p style=\"font-weight:400\">Reviews that rated how certain their evidence was reached a consistent point in massage’s favor: every conclusion rated moderate-certainty found massage associated with less pain, and a meta-analysis of 67 randomized trials found lower pain scores after massage than after sham massage, no massage or other approaches.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39008297/\">[1]</a><a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27165971/\">[2]</a></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"style\":{\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"400\"}}} -->\n<p style=\"font-weight:400\">The same reviews are careful about limits. None of the conclusions in the 2024 review was rated high-certainty, and conclusions backed by moderate- or high-certainty evidence that massage was better than other active approaches were rare. An earlier evidence map of 49 systematic reviews found that the high-quality reviews reached conclusions of low strength of evidence, because few large, rigorous studies had been done.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30892910/\">[11]</a> The meta-analysis’s authors made a strong recommendation only for the comparison with no massage and a weak one for sham massage and other approaches.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27165971/\">[2]</a> A 2015 review of 26 randomized trials with 2,565 people with muscle and joint problems found low-to-moderate-level evidence that massage reduced pain in the short term compared with no intervention in some groups, such as people with shoulder pain, but not in people with neck or low-back pain. Function improved in the short term for people with low-back and shoulder pain. Compared with another active approach, no clear benefit was seen.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26093806/\">[12]</a></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"style\":{\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"400\"}}} -->\n<p style=\"font-weight:400\">Massage is generally described as low risk, and where serious problems on record can be traced to who gave the massage, they are mostly tied to people who were not trained massage therapists. The U.S. National Center for Complementary and Integrative Health notes that the risk of harm appears to be low, although rare serious side effects such as a blood clot, nerve injury or bone fracture have been reported.<a href=\"https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/massage-therapy-what-you-need-to-know\">[9]</a> A 2014 review of 40 published reports found 138 adverse events over 11 years, mainly disc herniation, soft-tissue injury, nerve problems, spinal cord injury and tears in the neck arteries, with spinal manipulation inside a massage repeatedly linked to the serious ones. Massage therapists were the practitioners in only 8 of the 138 events (5.8 percent) and chiropractors in 33 (23.9 percent); the rest (70.3 percent) involved unregistered people or other health professionals, and the authors concluded that massage by nonprofessionals and forceful techniques is often associated with adverse events.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25197310/\">[13]</a> A 2003 review of 16 case reports and four case series likewise found that most adverse effects followed exotic techniques or massage by laypeople, with massage therapists rarely implicated, that serious effects were mostly tied to techniques other than Swedish massage, and concluded that serious events are probably true rarities.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12777645/\">[14]</a> The U.S. center also advises against using massage to put off seeing a health care provider about a medical problem. Taken together, in the reports that say who gave the massage, trained massage therapists were rarely involved: the serious injuries were mostly linked to people who were not massage therapists, including laypeople and unregistered practitioners, and to forceful or unusual techniques. Serious events are rare: I ask about your health, medications, pregnancy and recent injuries before each session, and you can ask for lighter pressure or a pause at any time.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n</details>\n<!-- /wp:details -->\n\n<!-- wp:details {\"style\":{\"border\":{\"width\":\"1px\",\"style\":\"solid\",\"color\":\"var(\\u002d\\u002dts-rule)\",\"radius\":\"4px\"},\"color\":{\"background\":\"var(\\u002d\\u002dts-panel)\"},\"spacing\":{\"padding\":{\"top\":\"0.85rem\",\"right\":\"1.1rem\",\"bottom\":\"0.85rem\",\"left\":\"1.1rem\"},\"margin\":{\"bottom\":\"0.7rem\"}},\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"600\"}}} -->\n<details class=\"wp-block-details has-border-color has-background\" style=\"border-color:var(--ts-rule);border-width:1px;border-style:solid;border-radius:4px;background-color:var(--ts-panel);padding-top:0.85rem;padding-right:1.1rem;padding-bottom:0.85rem;padding-left:1.1rem;margin-bottom:0.7rem;font-weight:600\"><summary>Neck, Shoulders and Back</summary>\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"style\":{\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"400\"}}} -->\n<p style=\"font-weight:400\">Several reviews report short-term gains from massage for neck, shoulder and back pain. A 2015 Cochrane review of 25 trials found that massage was better than no intervention for low-back pain in the short term.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26329399/\">[15]</a> A 2014 review of 20 trials found low-quality evidence that soft-tissue massage improved shoulder movement, pain and function compared with no intervention in the short term, right after the sessions ended.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22844035/\">[16]</a> In the 2024 Cochrane review of neck pain, a subgroup analysis favored higher doses of eight or more sessions of at least 30 minutes over four weeks.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38415786/\">[10]</a></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"style\":{\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"400\"}}} -->\n<p style=\"font-weight:400\">The same reviews also report the limits. The low-back review rated the quality of the evidence low to very low, found no long-term benefit over no treatment, and its authors said they had “very little confidence” in the results. No serious side effects were reported; the most common minor one was more pain, in 1.5 to 25 percent of people.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26329399/\">[15]</a> The 2024 neck review of 33 studies with 1,994 people found that, compared with a placebo massage, massage probably makes little or no difference to pain, function or quality of life at up to 12 weeks (low-certainty evidence), and adverse events were too poorly reported to judge.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38415786/\">[10]</a> In a trial of 80 adults with shoulder pain who were all doing exercises, adding four weeks of massage gave no extra benefit for pain, disability or movement, and a small difference in pain at 12 weeks favored exercise alone.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26023217/\">[17]</a> In 41 people with low-back pain who were all having physiotherapy, adding real massage or a placebo massage for three weeks both reduced pain and disability and improved mobility, and only the real-massage group improved on a quality-of-life measure.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40954653/\">[18]</a></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"style\":{\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"400\"}}} -->\n<p style=\"font-weight:400\">Smaller studies point the same encouraging way for tight shoulder muscles. In a crossover study of 17 healthy adults, muscle activity in the upper-shoulder muscles fell by 19 percent after a short, moderate-pressure massage of the shoulders and neck, compared with 1 percent after sitting quietly; the study measured muscle activity, not pain.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27977513/\">[19]</a> In a pilot study of 45 cardiac sonographers with work-related muscle discomfort, weekly 30-minute chair massages for 10 weeks, with or without stretching, were followed by some improvement in discomfort scores compared with no intervention, and the authors called for larger trials.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20846441/\">[20]</a></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n</details>\n<!-- /wp:details -->\n\n<!-- wp:details {\"style\":{\"border\":{\"width\":\"1px\",\"style\":\"solid\",\"color\":\"var(\\u002d\\u002dts-rule)\",\"radius\":\"4px\"},\"color\":{\"background\":\"var(\\u002d\\u002dts-panel)\"},\"spacing\":{\"padding\":{\"top\":\"0.85rem\",\"right\":\"1.1rem\",\"bottom\":\"0.85rem\",\"left\":\"1.1rem\"},\"margin\":{\"bottom\":\"0.7rem\"}},\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"600\"}}} -->\n<details class=\"wp-block-details has-border-color has-background\" style=\"border-color:var(--ts-rule);border-width:1px;border-style:solid;border-radius:4px;background-color:var(--ts-panel);padding-top:0.85rem;padding-right:1.1rem;padding-bottom:0.85rem;padding-left:1.1rem;margin-bottom:0.7rem;font-weight:600\"><summary>Exercise, Flexibility and Recovery</summary>\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"style\":{\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"400\"}}} -->\n<p style=\"font-weight:400\">Of the exercise outcomes reviewed, flexibility and soreness are where massage shows benefit. A 2020 review of 29 randomized studies with 1,012 people found small improvements in flexibility and in delayed-onset muscle soreness.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32426160/\">[3]</a> In 10 recreationally active women, 30 seconds of massage at the back of the thigh was followed by about a 7 percent larger hip-bend range than no massage, with no change in muscle tension or muscle activity.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20543728/\">[21]</a> In 52 healthy volunteers whose grip had been tired by exercise, five minutes of forearm and hand massage was followed by better grip performance than five minutes of rest or of passive joint movement; the study was small and measured only the minutes right afterward.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16398602/\">[22]</a></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"style\":{\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"400\"}}} -->\n<p style=\"font-weight:400\">The same 2020 review found no evidence that massage improved strength, jumping, sprinting, endurance or fatigue.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32426160/\">[3]</a> A 2016 review of 22 trials concluded that the effects of massage on performance recovery are rather small and partly unclear, and its authors questioned whether the limited effects justify using massage as a recovery method in competitive athletes.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26744335/\">[23]</a> In 62 healthy active students who had a 40-minute whole-body myofascial-release massage after hard exercise, massage was followed by lower muscle activity in a thigh muscle and a lower mood score for vigor than a sham session, which the authors said may reflect a transient loss of muscle strength or a state of relaxation.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/19123877/\">[24]</a> In people with sore leg muscles after exercise, manual massage, real diathermy (a heating device) and sham diathermy produced no clinically relevant differences in pain scores right after the session, although scores on a patient-rated scale of global change did differ between the groups.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31956433/\">[25]</a></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"style\":{\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"400\"}}} -->\n<p style=\"font-weight:400\">Where massage has been tested for short-term recovery after intense mixed training, the effects were medium in size: the 2016 review found medium positive effects after high-intensity mixed exercise, and shorter massage (5 to 12 minutes) tended to show larger effects than longer massage.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26744335/\">[23]</a></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n</details>\n<!-- /wp:details -->\n\n<!-- wp:details {\"style\":{\"border\":{\"width\":\"1px\",\"style\":\"solid\",\"color\":\"var(\\u002d\\u002dts-rule)\",\"radius\":\"4px\"},\"color\":{\"background\":\"var(\\u002d\\u002dts-panel)\"},\"spacing\":{\"padding\":{\"top\":\"0.85rem\",\"right\":\"1.1rem\",\"bottom\":\"0.85rem\",\"left\":\"1.1rem\"},\"margin\":{\"bottom\":\"0.7rem\"}},\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"600\"}}} -->\n<details class=\"wp-block-details has-border-color has-background\" style=\"border-color:var(--ts-rule);border-width:1px;border-style:solid;border-radius:4px;background-color:var(--ts-panel);padding-top:0.85rem;padding-right:1.1rem;padding-bottom:0.85rem;padding-left:1.1rem;margin-bottom:0.7rem;font-weight:600\"><summary>Stress, Relaxation and Well-Being</summary>\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"style\":{\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"400\"}}} -->\n<p style=\"font-weight:400\">Several studies point to short-term relaxation after massage. A 2004 meta-analysis of 37 randomized studies found that a single massage was followed by lower state anxiety, blood pressure and heart rate.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/14717648/\">[26]</a> In 60 healthy women, the two massage groups showed higher heart-rate variability than a resting group.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32901072/\">[4]</a> In 118 healthy adults who first took a stressful laboratory test, most people who had a real rhythmical massage described it as relaxing (82 to 100 percent, compared with 42 percent after a sham massage).<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26365448/\">[27]</a></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"style\":{\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"400\"}}} -->\n<p style=\"font-weight:400\">The picture is mixed, though. The same 2004 meta-analysis found no lower negative mood, cortisol or less immediate pain after a single massage,<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/14717648/\">[26]</a> and a 2011 review found that the effect on the stress hormone cortisol was generally very small and in most analyses not statistically distinguishable from zero.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21147413/\">[28]</a> In the 60 healthy women, relaxation rose and stress fell in the resting group too.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32901072/\">[4]</a> In the 118 healthy adults, a single rhythmical massage of about an hour was not followed by significantly better well-being scores or cortisol levels than a sham massage.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26365448/\">[27]</a> In 14 healthy men, a 20-minute massage and a 20-minute reading period were each followed by a rise in the hormone oxytocin, which led the researchers to suggest that touch is not needed for that rise.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18576922/\">[29]</a> In 15 healthy women, a single two-minute manual massage was not followed by significant differences in heart-rate variability or blood pressure compared with rest or a sham, although the authors noted changes of moderate to large size within the massage condition.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41154233/\">[30]</a> In 38 family caregivers at a rehabilitation hospital who had either one or three massages a week for two weeks, psychological symptom scores were lower afterward in both groups, but because everyone had massage the study cannot show how much of the change was due to the massage itself.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31003683/\">[6]</a></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"style\":{\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"400\"}}} -->\n<p style=\"font-weight:400\">Courses of massage have the more encouraging results: the largest effects in the 2004 meta-analysis came after a course of sessions rather than a single one, on measures of anxiety and mood.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/14717648/\">[26]</a> In a pilot study of 54 adults aged 60 and over, twice-weekly massage for four weeks was followed by better scores for well-being and perceived stress than guided relaxation.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/17709060/\">[5]</a> In a trial of 122 adults at an integrative-therapy clinic in Brazil, people had eight Anma (Japanese) massage sessions followed by rest, massage followed by Reiki, or no intervention. Stress fell more in the massage-plus-Reiki group than in the no-intervention group, and the authors called for a placebo group in later trials.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27901219/\">[7]</a> The same trial’s report on stress and quality of life (101 people analyzed) found that both massage groups did better than no intervention.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33053005/\">[8]</a></p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n</details>\n<!-- /wp:details -->\n\n<!-- wp:details {\"style\":{\"border\":{\"width\":\"1px\",\"style\":\"solid\",\"color\":\"var(\\u002d\\u002dts-rule)\",\"radius\":\"4px\"},\"color\":{\"background\":\"var(\\u002d\\u002dts-panel)\"},\"spacing\":{\"padding\":{\"top\":\"0.85rem\",\"right\":\"1.1rem\",\"bottom\":\"0.85rem\",\"left\":\"1.1rem\"},\"margin\":{\"bottom\":\"0.7rem\"}},\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"600\"}}} -->\n<details class=\"wp-block-details has-border-color has-background\" style=\"border-color:var(--ts-rule);border-width:1px;border-style:solid;border-radius:4px;background-color:var(--ts-panel);padding-top:0.85rem;padding-right:1.1rem;padding-bottom:0.85rem;padding-left:1.1rem;margin-bottom:0.7rem;font-weight:600\"><summary>Sources</summary>\n<!-- wp:paragraph {\"style\":{\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"400\"}}} -->\n<p style=\"font-weight:400\">Each number above links to the source. Titles are quoted as published.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list {\"ordered\":true,\"style\":{\"typography\":{\"fontWeight\":\"400\"}}} -->\n<ol class=\"wp-block-list\" style=\"font-weight:400\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Mak S, Allen J, Begashaw M, Miake-Lye I, Beroes-Severin J, De Vries G, Lawson E, Shekelle PG. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39008297/\">Use of Massage Therapy for Pain, 2018-2023: A Systematic Review</a>. JAMA Netw Open, 2024.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Crawford C, Boyd C, Paat CF, Price A, Xenakis L, Yang E, Zhang W; Evidence for Massage Therapy (EMT) Working Group. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27165971/\">The Impact of Massage Therapy on Function in Pain Populations-A Systematic Review and Meta-Analysis of Randomized Controlled Trials: Part I, Patients Experiencing Pain in the General Population</a>. Pain Med, 2016.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Davis HL, Alabed S, Chico TJA. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32426160/\">Effect of sports massage on performance and recovery: a systematic review and meta-analysis</a>. BMJ Open Sport Exerc Med, 2020.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Meier M, Unternaehrer E, Dimitroff SJ, Benz ABE, Bentele UU, Schorpp SM, Wenzel M, Pruessner JC. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32901072/\">Standardized massage interventions as protocols for the induction of psychophysiological relaxation in the laboratory: a block randomized, controlled trial</a>. Sci Rep, 2020.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Sharpe PA, Williams HG, Granner ML, Hussey JR. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/17709060/\">A randomised study of the effects of massage therapy compared to guided relaxation on well-being and stress perception among older adults</a>. Complement Ther Med, 2007.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Williams NA, Burnfield JM, Springer P, Wolf K, Buster T. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31003683/\">Therapeutic massage to enhance family caregivers' well-being in a rehabilitation hospital</a>. Complement Ther Clin Pract, 2019.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Kurebayashi LF, Turrini RN, Souza TP, Takiguchi RS, Kuba G, Nagumo MT. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27901219/\">Massage and Reiki used to reduce stress and anxiety: Randomized Clinical Trial</a>. Rev Lat Am Enfermagem, 2016.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Kurebayashi LFS, Gnatta JR, Kuba G, Giaponesi ALL, Souza TPB, Turrini RNT. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33053005/\">Massage and Reiki to reduce stress and improve quality of life: a randomized clinical trial</a>. Rev Esc Enferm USP, 2020.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">National Center for Complementary and Integrative Health (NIH). <a href=\"https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/massage-therapy-what-you-need-to-know\">Massage Therapy: What You Need To Know</a>. Last updated May 2019.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Gross AR, Lee H, Ezzo J, Chacko N, Gelley G, Forget M, Morien A, Graham N, Santaguida PL, Rice M, Dixon C. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38415786/\">Massage for neck pain</a>. Cochrane Database Syst Rev, 2024 (searches to October 2023).</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Miake-Lye IM, Mak S, Lee J, Luger T, Taylor SL, Shanman R, Beroes-Severin JM, Shekelle PG. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30892910/\">Massage for Pain: An Evidence Map</a>. J Altern Complement Med, 2019.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Bervoets DC, Luijsterburg PA, Alessie JJ, Buijs MJ, Verhagen AP. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26093806/\">Massage therapy has short-term benefits for people with common musculoskeletal disorders compared to no treatment: a systematic review</a>. J Physiother, 2015.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Yin P, Gao N, Wu J, Litscher G, Xu S. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25197310/\">Adverse events of massage therapy in pain-related conditions: a systematic review</a>. Evid Based Complement Alternat Med, 2014.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Ernst E. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12777645/\">The safety of massage therapy</a>. Rheumatology (Oxford), 2003.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Furlan AD, Giraldo M, Baskwill A, Irvin E, Imamura M. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26329399/\">Massage for low-back pain</a>. Cochrane Database Syst Rev, 2015.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">van den Dolder PA, Ferreira PH, Refshauge KM. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22844035/\">Effectiveness of soft tissue massage and exercise for the treatment of non-specific shoulder pain: a systematic review with meta-analysis</a>. Br J Sports Med, 2014.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">van den Dolder PA, Ferreira PH, Refshauge KM. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26023217/\">Effectiveness of Soft Tissue Massage for Nonspecific Shoulder Pain: Randomized Controlled Trial</a>. Phys Ther, 2015.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Ucurum SG, Celenay ST, Keskin M, Kaya DO. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40954653/\">Short-term effects of classical massage added to a conventional physiotherapy on non-specific chronic low back Pain: A randomized, placebo-controlled study</a>. J Bodyw Mov Ther, 2025.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Domingo AR, Diek M, Goble KM, Maluf KS, Goble DJ, Baweja HS. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27977513/\">Short-duration therapeutic massage reduces postural upper trapezius muscle activity</a>. Neuroreport, 2017.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Engen DJ, Wahner-Roedler DL, Nadolny AM, Persinger CM, Oh JK, Spittell PC, Loehrer LL, Cha SS, Bauer BA. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20846441/\">The effect of chair massage on muscular discomfort in cardiac sonographers: a pilot study</a>. BMC Complement Altern Med, 2010.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Huang SY, Di Santo M, Wadden KP, Cappa DF, Alkanani T, Behm DG. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20543728/\">Short-duration massage at the hamstrings musculotendinous junction induces greater range of motion</a>. J Strength Cond Res, 2010.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Brooks CP, Woodruff LD, Wright LL, Donatelli R. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16398602/\">The immediate effects of manual massage on power-grip performance after maximal exercise in healthy adults</a>. J Altern Complement Med, 2005.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Poppendieck W, Wegmann M, Ferrauti A, Kellmann M, Pfeiffer M, Meyer T. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26744335/\">Massage and Performance Recovery: A Meta-Analytical Review</a>. Sports Med, 2016.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Arroyo-Morales M, Olea N, Martínez MM, Hidalgo-Lozano A, Ruiz-Rodríguez C, Díaz-Rodríguez L. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/19123877/\">Psychophysiological effects of massage-myofascial release after exercise: a randomized sham-control study</a>. J Altern Complement Med, 2008.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Visconti L, Forni C, Coser R, Trucco M, Magnano E, Capra G. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31956433/\">Comparison of the effectiveness of manual massage, long-wave diathermy, and sham long-wave diathermy for the management of delayed-onset muscle soreness: a randomized controlled trial</a>. Arch Physiother, 2020.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Moyer CA, Rounds J, Hannum JW. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/14717648/\">A meta-analysis of massage therapy research</a>. Psychol Bull, 2004.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Kanitz JL, Reif M, Rihs C, Krause I, Seifert G. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26365448/\">A randomised, controlled, single-blinded study on the impact of a single rhythmical massage (anthroposophic medicine) on well-being and salivary cortisol in healthy adults</a>. Complement Ther Med, 2015.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Moyer CA, Seefeldt L, Mann ES, Jackley LM. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21147413/\">Does massage therapy reduce cortisol? A comprehensive quantitative review</a>. J Bodyw Mov Ther, 2011.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Bello D, White-Traut R, Schwertz D, Pournajafi-Nazarloo H, Carter CS. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18576922/\">An exploratory study of neurohormonal responses of healthy men to massage</a>. J Altern Complement Med, 2008.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item -->\n\n<!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><cite class=\"ts-source\">Monteiro ER, Pescatello LS, Garcia GH, de Meirelles AG, de Oliveira F, de Souza RC, Nogueira LAC, Lopes AJ, Moreira-Gonçalves D. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41154233/\">Upper Cervical Manipulation and Manual Massage Do Not Modulate Sympatho-Vagal Balance or Blood Pressure in Women: A Randomized, Placebo-Controlled Clinical Trial</a>. Healthcare (Basel), 2025.</cite></li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ol>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n</details>\n<!-- /wp:details -->\n",
